/P 146 0 R
/E (Through discussions and a community rally, participants gained awareness about the dangers of improper waste disposal, particularly in areas near creeks where the risk of water contamination and disease is high. Women expressed a commitment to adopting better sanitation practices, such as waste segregation, reducing plastic use, and avoiding open dumping. Many also committed to sharing their knowledge with neighbours and family members, indicating a growing sense of community responsibility and ownership.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
376 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 146 0 R
/E (In Saphale, the session reached out to tribal communities and emphasized sustainable waste management practices, including the use of eco-friendly soak pits, plastic waste reduction, and water conservation. Women were encouraged to participate in community-based decision-making and take initiative in local sanitation efforts, further strengthening their roles as change-makers. These sessions not only improved awareness and capacity-building but also served as catalysts for long-term behaviour change. By involving women in planning and implementing waste management strategies, Sakhya is promoting cleaner, healthier communities and laying the groundwork for sustainable environmental practices.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [1]
>>
